.TH MESH 2
.SH NAME
mesh: parse \- a little language for describing a domain grid
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "mesh.m";
mesh := load Mesh Mesh->PATH;

(g, err) := mesh->parse("mesh 64x64 domain 1x1 bc periodic");
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mesh
parses one line describing a rectangular grid \(em resolution, physical
domain size, and boundary condition \(em into a
.B Grid
holding exactly the geometry half of
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.BR Field .
It is split out as its own module, rather than living inside
.IR pde (2)
itself, so any future problem class describes its domain the same way
instead of re-deriving
.RI \(lq NXxNY " domain " WxH " bc " ... \(rq
parsing on its own: a mesh is infrastructure every problem class
shares, the equation on top of it is each class's own concern.
.PP
The grammar is one line:
.EX
mesh NXxNY [domain WxH] [bc clamp|periodic|zero]
.EE
.I NXxNY
gives the resolution; both dimensions must be at least 2.
.I domain
gives the physical width and height, defaulting to
.B 1x1
if omitted \(em cell size is then
.IR width / NX
by
.IR height / NY .
.I bc
gives the boundary condition (see
.IR pde (2)
for what each mode means), defaulting to
.BR clamp .
.PP
.B Parse
returns
.RI ( nil ", " error )
on a malformed line, describing what was wrong; a
.IR pde (2)
consumer such as
.B newproblem
passes that error straight back to its own caller unchanged.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/mesh.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/mesh.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pde (2),
.IR sh-mesh (1)
